1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:14,500
I'm building a Hermes skill that turns prose into a film storyboard.

2
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:24,000
You write a scene. Kimi K2.5 parses it into six shots
with lenses, camera moves and eye-line.

3
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:34,000
The local SVG renderer draws the board stroke-by-stroke,
in the browser.

4
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Every frame is metadata plus a rendered primitive.
Rain, neon, silhouette, focus ring, eye-line marker —

5
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:50,000
all defined by the Scene JSON schema. No diffusion,
no artifacts. The final board stays editable as vector.

6
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:56,500
I revise one frame — "more Hitchcock, low angle,
hard shadow, silhouette".

7
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Hermes extracts a generalised director rule and saves it.
The next matching scene is already shot that way.

8
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:13,000
Here's a second scene. Nobody requested the same style —
but Hermes applies the rule on its own.

9
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:21,000
Low angle. Shadow. Silhouette on the landing.
Memory persists across scenes.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Tags matched. Rule applied. No re-prompting.

11
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:33,000
Every artifact is inspectable. Rule, agent trace,
deterministic patch validation.

12
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:42,000
Hermes writes these same files to disk when the skill runs locally.
Scene JSON, critique patches, character bible, director memory.

13
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:48,000
Kimi K2.5 has three roles. Parses the prose.
Critiques the rendered board.

14
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:54,000
Extracts director memory. And it stays grounded —
no plot hallucinations reach the final artifact.

15
00:01:54,500 --> 00:02:06,000
Not SaaS. It's a local Hermes skill —
three commands and it's yours.

16
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:13,000
Hermes doesn't just draw the scene.
It learns how you direct the next one.
